From bb734f5c1c61900ba20364a0cc03804100959af6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rafia Sabih <rafia.sabih@cybertec.at>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 13:01:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH v17 2/2] postgres_fdw: Add streaming_fetch option for
 cursor-free fetching

postgres_fdw uses cursors to fetch tuples from the remote server
incrementally. While this works correctly, cursors prevent the remote
side from using parallel query execution, which can significantly limit
performance for large scans.

This adds a new boolean option streaming_fetch, available at both the
server and table level and defaulting to false. Instead of a cursor,
the scan is run with libpq's chunked-rows mode using fetch_size as the
chunk size, so the remote query is free to use parallel execution.
Asynchronous execution is not supported in this mode and is disabled
automatically when streaming_fetch is enabled.

A connection can hold only one chunked-rows result at a time. When a
second scan begins on the same connection while another is still in
progress, the remaining tuples of the active scan are drained into a
tuplestore (bounded by work_mem) and replayed when execution returns to
that scan, preserving correct results across nested or interleaved
scans without an additional connection. If a drain is interrupted by an
error, the partially filled tuplestore is marked so that a later read
raises an error instead of returning silently short results, since the
drain may have aborted only a subtransaction.

A scan that stops before reading all rows, such as an un-pushed-down
LIMIT or a rescan, must drain and discard the rest of the remote result
before the connection can be reused, and a parameterized rescan re-runs
the remote query from scratch, so streaming_fetch can be slower than
the cursor path when few rows are read.

Incidentally, pgfdw_exec_query() now always drains an in-progress
streaming_fetch scan before sending its query and requires a non-NULL
connection state, and fetch_more_data() counts a fetch on entry rather
than on success. create_cursor()'s parameter setup and connection
draining are split out into prepare_query() and shared with the new init_scan().

Original idea: Bernd Helmle
Key suggestions and review: Robert Haas
